#: ../../README.md:1
msgid "morpheus-docs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:2
msgid ""
"This is the document repository for [Morpheus "
"Data](https://www.morpheusdata.com \"Morpheus Homepage\").  All content "
"is automatically published to the [Morpheus Docs "
"Site](https://docs.morpheusdata.com \"Morpheus Docs\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:4
msgid ""
"The content of these docs is open source.  This means you are free to "
"contribute to them by issuing pull requests from a fork."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:6
msgid "Contributing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:7
msgid ""
"The standard flow for contributing to a project such as this is to fork "
"the repo & clone locally, create an upstream remote and sync your local "
"copy before you branch, branch for each separate piece of work, do work "
"including writing good commit messages, push to your origin repo, create "
"a new PR in GitHub, and respond to any code review feedback."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:9
msgid "Step 1: Fork"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:10
msgid ""
"Firstly you need a local fork of the the project, so go ahead and press "
"the “fork” button in GitHub. This will create a copy of the repository in"
" your own GitHub account and you’ll see a note that it’s been forked "
"underneath the project name: ![alt text](/images/forked.png?raw=true "
"\"Forked Project\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:10 ../../README.md:63 ../../README.md:68
msgid "alt text"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:10
msgid ".. image:: images/forked.png?raw=true"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:13
msgid ""
"Now you need a copy locally, so find the “SSH clone URL” in the right "
"hand column and use that to clone locally using a terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:14
msgid "$ git clone git@github.com:tadamhicks/morpheus-docs.git\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:18
msgid "Change into the new project’s directory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:19
msgid "$ cd morpheus-docs\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:23
msgid ""
"Finally, in this stage, you need to set up a new remote that points to "
"the original project so that you can grab any changes and bring them into"
" your local copy. Firstly clock on the link to the original repository – "
"it’s labeled “Forked from” at the top of the GitHub page. This takes you "
"back to the projects main GitHub page, so you can find the “SSH clone "
"URL” and use it to create the new remote, which we’ll call upstream."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:24
msgid "$ git remote add upstream git@github.com:gomorpheus/morpheus-docs.git\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:28
msgid "You now have two remotes for this project on disk:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:30
msgid ""
"1. origin which points to your GitHub fork of the project. You can read "
"and write to this remote.\n"
"2. upstream which points to the main project’s GitHub repository. You can"
" only read from this remote.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:33
msgid "Step 2: Do some work"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:34
msgid ""
"This is the fun bit where you get to contribute to the project. It’s "
"usually best to start by fixing a bug that is either annoying you or "
"you’ve found on the project’s issue tracker."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:35
msgid "BRANCH!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:36
msgid ""
"**The number one rule is to put each piece of work on its own branch.** "
"The general rule for most dev work is that if you are bug fixing, then "
"branch from master and if you are adding a new feature then branch from "
"develop. If the project only has a master branch, the branch from that. "
"In the case of our documentation, branch most new content from the branch"
" named for the lastest version number (such as 6.1.2). If your new "
"content is relevant to a specific past version, you can branch from that "
"version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:38
msgid ""
"For this example, we’ll branch from master though most new content should"
" be branched from a version-numbered branch:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:39
msgid ""
"$ git checkout master\n"
"$ git pull upstream master && git push origin master\n"
"$ git checkout -b hotfix/readme-update\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:45
msgid ""
"Firstly we ensure we’re on the master branch. Then the git pull command "
"will sync our local copy with the upstream project and the git push syncs"
" it to our forked GitHub project. Finally we create our new branch. You "
"can name your branch whatever you like, but it helps for it to be "
"meaningful. Including the issue number is usually helpful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:47
msgid "Now you can do your work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:49
msgid ""
"**Ensure that you only fix the thing you’re working on. Do not be tempted"
" to fix some other things that you see along the way, including "
"formatting issues, as your PR will probably be rejected.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:51
msgid ""
"Make sure that you commit in logical blocks. Each commit message should "
"be sane. Read Tim Pope’s [A Note About Git Commit "
"Messages](http://tbaggery.com/2008/04/19/a-note-about-git-commit-"
"messages.html \"A Note About Git Commit Messages\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:53
msgid "Step 3: Create the PR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:54
msgid ""
"To create a PR you need to push your branch to the origin remote and then"
" press some buttons on GitHub."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:56
msgid "To push a new branch:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:57
msgid "$ git push -u origin hotfix/readme-update\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:61
msgid ""
"This will create the branch on your GitHub project. The -u flag links "
"this branch with the remote one, so that in the future, you can simply "
"type git push origin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:63
msgid ""
"Swap back to the browser and navigate to your fork of the project "
"(https://github.com/tadamhicks/morpheus-docs in my case) and you’ll see "
"that your new branch is listed at the top with a handy “Compare & pull "
"request” button: ![alt text](/images/pullrequest.png?raw=true \"Pull "
"Request\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:63
msgid ".. image:: images/pullrequest.png?raw=true"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:66
msgid "Go ahead and press the button!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:68
msgid ""
"On this page, ensure that the “base fork” points to the correct "
"repository and branch. Then ensure that you provide a good, succinct "
"title for your pull request and explain why you have created it in the "
"description box. Add any relevant issue numbers if you have them. ![alt "
"text](/images/prconfirm.png?raw=true \"Pull Request Confirmation\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:68
msgid ".. image:: images/prconfirm.png?raw=true"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:71
msgid ""
"If you scroll down a bit, you’ll see a diff of your changes. **Double "
"check that it contains what you expect.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:73
msgid ""
"Once you are happy, press the “Create pull request” button and you’re "
"done."
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:75
msgid "Step 4: Review by the maintainers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../README.md:76
msgid ""
"For your work to be integrated into the project, the maintainers will "
"review your work and either request changes or merge it."
msgstr ""

